{
  "notes": [
    {
      "day": "October 25th",
      "topics": [
        {
          "title": "Two pointers",
          "category": "Two Pointers",
          "patterns": [
            {
              "name": "Converging pointers",
              "text": "One pointer starts at the left, one at the right, and they move toward each other based on a comparison."
            },
            {
              "name": "Parallel pointers",
              "text": "Both pointers move forward, either one fast and one slow, or each pointer walking through its own list."
            },
            {
              "name": "Trigger-based pointers",
              "text": "The first pointer runs ahead, and the second only moves when something \"triggers\" it: a gap is reached or a constraint is broken. Most sliding window problems fall here."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Arrays & Hashing",
          "category": "Arrays & Hashing",
          "patterns": [
            {
              "name": "\"Have I seen this before?\" (hash set)",
              "text": "You store what you've seen in a set so checking for something takes `O(1)` instead of scanning again."
            },
            {
              "name": "Counting frequencies (hash map: item → count)",
              "text": "You count how many times each thing appears, then compare or sort those counts."
            },
            {
              "name": "Complement lookup (hash map: value → index)",
              "text": "As you go, you store what you've seen and ask whether the piece you need is already in the map."
            },
            {
              "name": "Array tricks (no hashing, but same section)",
              "text": "Product of array except self: build a prefix product pass left → right, then a suffix pass right → left. Prefix/suffix thinking shows up constantly.\n\nEncode & decode strings: a design problem. Prefix each string with its length & a delimiter so any character can safely appear inside the strings."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "day": "October 26th",
      "topics": [
        {
          "title": "Sliding window",
          "category": "Sliding Window",
          "patterns": [
            {
              "name": "Variable-size window (expand, then shrink when broken)",
              "text": "The right pointer always moves forward to grow the window. The left pointer only moves when the window breaks a rule, and then it shrinks until the window is valid again."
            },
            {
              "name": "Fixed-size window (slide one step at a time)",
              "text": "The window size never changes. Each step, add the new element on the right and remove the old one on the left."
            },
            {
              "name": "Window with a monotonic deque",
              "text": "You need the max or min of every window quickly, so you keep a deque in sorted order."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Binary search",
          "category": "Binary Search",
          "patterns": [
            {
              "name": "Classic search on sorted data",
              "text": "The array is sorted, so you cut the search space in half each step."
            },
            {
              "name": "Binary search on the answer",
              "text": "You're searching a range of possible answers, not an array. For each guess, you check \"does this work?\" and go lower or higher."
            },
            {
              "name": "Modified binary search (rotated arrays)",
              "text": "The array is sorted but rotated, so you first figure out which half is properly sorted, then decide where to go."
            },
            {
              "name": "Searching for the closest match",
              "text": "You're not looking for an exact value but for the largest one that's still ≤ a target."
            },
            {
              "name": "Partition search"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "day": "October 27th",
      "topics": [
        {
          "title": "Linked lists",
          "category": "Linked List",
          "patterns": [
            {
              "name": "Rewiring pointers (reversal)",
              "text": "You change where each node's `next` points. The core skill is saving `next` before you overwrite it so you don't lose the rest of the list."
            },
            {
              "name": "Fast/slow and gap pointers",
              "text": "Two pointers move through the list at different speeds or with a fixed distance between them."
            },
            {
              "name": "Building a new list with a dummy node",
              "text": "You create a fake starting node so you never have to handle \"what if the list is empty\" separately."
            },
            {
              "name": "Linked list + hash map",
              "text": "The list alone isn't enough, so a hash map gives you instant access to nodes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Stacks",
          "category": "Stack",
          "patterns": [
            {
              "name": "Matching the most recent thing",
              "text": "A stack remembers what you opened last, so you can match it when it closes."
            },
            {
              "name": "Stack as a design or calculation tool"
            },
            {
              "name": "Backtracking (building choices step by step)"
            },
            {
              "name": "Monotonic stack",
              "text": "You keep the stack in increasing or decreasing order, popping anything that breaks the order. This is how you solve \"next greater/smaller element\" problems in `O(n)`."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "day": "October 28th",
      "topics": [
        {
          "title": "Trees",
          "category": "Trees",
          "patterns": [
            {
              "name": "Basic DFS recursion",
              "text": "You ask each child for an answer, then combine the two answers at the current node. The base case is almost always `if not node: return ...`"
            },
            {
              "name": "Bottom-up with an extra answer on the side",
              "text": "The recursion returns one thing (usually height), but the answer you actually want is something else, which you update along the way."
            },
            {
              "name": "Top-down: passing info down to children",
              "text": "Instead of collecting answers from below, you send information down as you go."
            },
            {
              "name": "BFS (level by level)",
              "text": "Use a queue to visit the tree one level at a time."
            },
            {
              "name": "Using the BST property",
              "text": "In a binary search tree, left < node < right, so you often know which way to go without checking both sides."
            },
            {
              "name": "Building and rebuilding trees"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Tries",
          "category": "Tries",
          "sub": "Tree where each node is a letter.",
          "patterns": [
            {
              "name": "The basic trie"
            },
            {
              "name": "Trie + DFS"
            },
            {
              "name": "Trie + backtracking"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "day": "October 29th",
      "topics": [
        {
          "title": "Backtracking",
          "category": "Backtracking",
          "patterns": [
            {
              "name": "Subsets (include it or skip it)",
              "text": "For each element, you make one decision: take it or don't."
            },
            {
              "name": "Combinations (pick toward a target)",
              "text": "You build combinations that add up to a target, passing a start index so you never go backwards and create repeats."
            },
            {
              "name": "Permutations (order matters)",
              "text": "Every arrangement counts, so instead of a start index you track which elements are already used."
            },
            {
              "name": "Building strings piece by piece"
            },
            {
              "name": "Grids and boards with constraints"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Heap / priority queue",
          "category": "Heap / Priority Queue",
          "patterns": [
            {
              "name": "Top K with a heap of size K",
              "text": "To find the K largest, keep a min-heap of size K. If it grows past K, pop the smallest. What's left is the top K."
            },
            {
              "name": "Simulation (keep grabbing the biggest)",
              "text": "You repeatedly take the most important item, process it, and maybe push something back."
            },
            {
              "name": "Merging multiple sorted lists"
            },
            {
              "name": "Two heaps"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "day": "October 30th",
      "topics": [
        {
          "title": "Graphs",
          "category": "Graphs",
          "patterns": [
            {
              "name": "Grid DFS/BFS (flood fill)",
              "text": "The grid is the graph: each cell is a node, and its up/down/left/right neighbours are its edges. You start from a cell and spread out, marking cells as visited."
            },
            {
              "name": "Multi-source BFS",
              "text": "Several starting points spread at the same time, so you put all of them in the queue before you begin."
            },
            {
              "name": "Copying a graph"
            },
            {
              "name": "Topological sort (directed graphs, dependencies)",
              "text": "When some things must come before others, you need an order that respects every dependency, and a cycle means there's no valid order."
            },
            {
              "name": "Union-Find and connectivity (undirected graphs)",
              "text": "You figure out which nodes are connected to each other. Union-Find merges groups and quickly tells you whether two nodes are already in the same group."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
